Z||000000||burdell1||02-16-2000||07:00 PM||||I was going to replace the seat covers on my Fiero and if other people had similar areas where their seats rip. Mine ripped on the seat itself, right about in the middle of the seat. It also ripped on the back portion on the left side towards the bottom. If so, what are some preventive measures one can take to prevent this....||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||02-16-2000||07:44 PM||||I found this about seats on Ed Park's website:<BR>                                         <B>SEATS RIPS</B><P>Q: Hello, I'm new to this site and appreciate all the e-mails! Anyways, I've had two Fiero's and have experienced in both of them rips in the seat fabric caused by (not sex! those cars are too small!), but from just every day wear and tear from getting in and out of them. My questions are: Has any one else experienced this? How do I repair some thing like this w/out spending a lot of $, and, How do I prevent more damage?<P>A: Both rear seat back coverings are identical, side to side, and can be switched. Both bottom cushion coverings, while not totally identical can also be switched. These are the "skins", not the actual cushions themselves. So, you can use a nearly always good passenger seat, to recover a drivers seat.<P>To avoid future problems, just slide the drivers seat to the rearmost position, and step out of the car, instead of sliding out with the seat in a forward position. Then, just "plop" in, and pull the seat forward when you are ready to go again. Just a habit to get into.||||reg||1
